I was working with Kai Sterker (well, asking for help) on a floating 
point exception in the Adonthell program, when she noticed this 
interesting feature: 

	[superluser@trystero superluser]$ cat fpe.cc 
	#include <stdio.h>

	    int main(void)
	    {
	      int x = (int) (4/(float)0);
	      printf ("x = %i\n", x);
	    }

	[superluser@trystero superluser]$ g++ fpe.cc 
	fpe.cc: In function `int main()':
	fpe.cc:5: warning: division by zero in `4 / 0.'
	[superluser@trystero superluser]$ ./a.out 
	x = 0

This is gcc version 3.2 20021115 (Red Hat Linux 8.0 3.2-14).  Should 
this be normal behavior?  Is this the expected output of standard C++?

