> updated rpm files.
> 
> The easiest way to install the updated rpms is with:
> 
>    rpm -Fvh *

Close, but sometimes you need to be a bit more careful than that.  There
are, for example, some more architecture specific rpms and, if your
processor fits, you do not want to give to 'rpm -Fvh' at the same time
both
    glibc-2.2.4-30.1.alphaev56.rpm
and
    glibc-2.2.4-30.1.alpha.rpm

as this will not work.  Currently older version of packages, if present,
were (mostly) moved to 'archives' but in the past they were "mixed in"
together and this may happen in the future or you may create such
situation locally if you will fetch new packages without cleaning up
older versions you got previously.  Something else to watch for.
Feeding with * to rpm a non-rpm file is also not recommended. :-)

A simple way out is to use 'rpm -Fvh *.rpm' in a directory with only
those packages you are really trying to install.  A bunch of links will
work just fine.

To make things even more interesting the server mentioned above at this
moment offers updates upto 'gdk-pixbuf' (Nov  7 17:14) while another
server listed on Compaq web pages, i.e.
ftp://alpha.crl.dec.com/pub/linux/redhat/7.2-alpha/updates/ has also
updates to 'mozilla' and 'galeon'.  Another time this can be the other
way around. :-)

Hm, even with two versions of mozilla (0.9.9-12.7.2 and 1.0.1-2.7.2),
which gives extra eleven unwanted packages, I can see on the later
server only 139 alpha.rpm files and not mentioned 145.  George,
something else is missing in both places?

  Michal




