On Thu, Dec 05, 2002 at 12:48:43AM -0600, G.L. Grobe wrote:
> 
> ... meaning ... when I view the CD, should I just see the file name of the
> ISO copied onto it or should I actually see a directory structure layout of
> the contents?

'ls -aF', or equivalent,  should show you the following on this disk:

./            COPYING          RELEASE-NOTES  TRANS.TBL  etc/
../           README           RPM-GPG-KEY    autorun*   images/
.disc1-alpha  README-Alpha-HW  RedHat/        boot/      kernels/

> Because right now all I have is a single file name as follows
> that's just under 659 when viewing the cd contents.
> 
> RedHat-7.2-alpha-20020627-disk1.iso

Which means that your program created a file system with one '...1.iso'
file in it and wrote that to CD.  That is not it.  Images you got are
meant to be blasted on CDs "as is".  Either wrong option in your CD
writer or wrong program.  No wonder that you are unable to boot this.
As opposed to the original image, which contains also some tings
_outside_ of a file system,  what you have now is not bootable.

  Michal




