On Thu, 5 Dec 2002, Andrew A. Gill wrote:

>Date: Thu, 5 Dec 2002 19:11:13 -0500 (EST)
>From: Andrew A. Gill <superluser@mail.isc.rit.edu>
>To: axp-list@redhat.com
>Content-Type: TEXT/PLAIN; charset=US-ASCII
>List-Id: Linux and Red Hat on Alpha processors <axp-list.redhat.com>
>Subject: Re: kernel-drm 4.2.99.2
>
>On Thu, 5 Dec 2002, Michal Jaegermann antagonized the horn and now...
>
>> On Thu, Dec 05, 2002 at 02:54:03PM -0500, Andrew A. Gill wrote:
>> > So I've decided to upgrade X yet again.  But now it's asking for 
>> > kernel-drm 4.2.99.2.
>> 
>> You can ignore that (with '--nodeps' if you are using rpm) but drm
>> will not work.  X should regardless.
>> 
>> > I really don't want to compile my own kernel,
>> 
>> You do not really have much choice if you insist on the latest drm
>> support.
>
>But I really don't want that.  I just want X to work.  With a little bit 
>of OpenGL.  I really would rather not have a bleeding edge snapshot of 
>X, but Sawfish ain't working, and upgrading it requires upgrading X, and 
>Enlightenment is going cuckoo, now.
>
>I can't figure it out.  I'd love to have XFree 4.1.0-25 work, but I 
>can't seem to get it to.
>
>All the icons are in the right places, but the places are somehow 
>showing up on a 1/4 scale.  What the hell is going on?

You have no choice.  New XFree86 releases *ALWAYS* come with new 
kernel DRM requirements.  You either completely disable DRI in 
your X config and have 2D-only with the new X, or you upgrade to 
the new kernel DRM and have 3D as well.  If you upgrade X anyway, 
and keep DRI enabled, and do not upgrade DRM, then you end up 
with random behaviour depending on how DRM has changed.  With 
some hardware it might just magically work perhaps, but the usual 
case is random lockups and data corruption.

Hence, the requirement to upgrade kernel DRM.  The Red Hat kernel 
being the place where kernel modules reside, is where the 
kernel-drm virtual provides resides also.  If you are using my 
CVS XFree86 RPMs, you will need to also use rawhide kernel which 
includes the new DRM.

Other alternatives include:

1) Extracting the DRM source code from our kernel (it is modified 
   from the upstream one to fix bugs) and compiling it yourself.

2) Not using DRI, and thus losing 3D acceleration, and ending up 
   with software (slow) OpenGL.

Hope this helps.


-- 
