On Fri, 6 Dec 2002, Andrew A. Gill wrote:

>> Hence, the requirement to upgrade kernel DRM.  The Red Hat kernel 
>> being the place where kernel modules reside, is where the 
>> kernel-drm virtual provides resides also.  If you are using my 
>> CVS XFree86 RPMs, you will need to also use rawhide kernel which 
>> includes the new DRM.
>
>But I did upgrade the kernel.  It's 2.4.9-40.  XFree86-4.1.0-25 liked it 
>just fine.  But somehow I get crap all over the left side of the screen.  
>And missing objects on the right.  And it looks like the crap on the 
>left side is just what should be on the right, except 1/4 the size.

The Red Hat kernel contains the virtual provides "kernel-drm" for 
the versions of DRM that it supports.  The Red Hat XFree86 
packages have a virtual Requires that require the matching 
kernel-drm that our kernel provides.  You must make sure that 
they match by installing a Red Hat kernel that contains the DRM 
version that the XFree86 release is using.

>I really don't want to upgrade to an even less stable version just to 
>see if it works, but I don't see that I have much choice.

No, you're aparently talking about XFree86 4.1.0, whereas I am 
talking about CVS XFree86 in rawhide.  The rawhide X requires the 
rawhide kernel.  The Red Hat Linux 7.2/Alpha X, requires the Red 
Hat Linux supplied kernel for Alpha also.

>What's going on with 4.1.0-25, and how do I fix it?

Install a Red Hat kernel that shipped with the OS or was 
released as erratum for the OS.




