> But I did upgrade the kernel.  It's 2.4.9-40.  XFree86-4.1.0-25 liked
> it 
> just fine.  But somehow I get crap all over the left side of the
> screen.  
> And missing objects on the right.  And it looks like the crap on the 
> left side is just what should be on the right, except 1/4 the size.
> 
> I really don't want to upgrade to an even less stable version just to
> see if it works, but I don't see that I have much choice.
> 
> What's going on with 4.1.0-25, and how do I fix it?
> 
> -- 
> |Andrew A. Gill                       |I posted to Silent-Tristero
> 
> That's what I really want.
> 

Re rpms - the packager has to make some choices, the package
has dependencies. You have to conform to the assumptions made
by the packager.
If you want to mix and match thats fine but now you will have
to deal with the sources (or source rpms). It is perfectly
possible to take the 'drm' module from the XFree86 4.2 source
and move it to the kernel tree and make a 4.2 DRM module
compatible with your own kernel (I have XFree86 4.2 with DRI
working on a 2.4.8 kernel) If you want to experiment thats great
(but you may need to learn how to compile the kernel).
Re bugs - If you are on the cutting edge you are going to find bugs,
thats great too, test everything, document what you did and report
back to the maintainer. XFree86 is a big complicated thing, video
artifacts can often be fixed by changing the config files, try
editing /etc/X11/XF86Config-4 and try different drivers, modes
and depths. You can also install the source rpm for XFree86
and read the driver code. It often has hints about different cards,

hope this is helpful,
Stephen Lewis




