On Sat, Dec 07, 2002 at 10:55:44PM -0600, G.L. Grobe wrote:
> 
> But then right at about half way through the install, it just stops. This is
> a PWS500a.

Usually "text" mode installations are more robust.  You avoid possible
troubles with a framebuffer support for your particular video/PCI bus
combo.  You can edit etc/aboot.conf on your floppy to add a line with
options you desire.  Less opportunities to mistype something later.

Another possibility is to install a "minimal system" (pick up a "Custom"
installation and deselect _everything_ which is there to deselect;
a basic system will be installed anyway) and worry about fleshing it
up later when you have already something to boot.  It is even possible
to use "Update" for that although it may have the same troubles you
are seeing right now; still you can run 'rpm' just from a command
line.

If you will hit Alt-Ctrl-F2 (...F3,F4,F1) you will get text screens, one
with a shell running, and often messages there which may shed some light
on why something is unhappy.  There also should be a log from your
current installation in /tmp/syslog, IIRC.  It is not difficult to save
it on a mounted floppy for a later perusal (your boot floppy should do,
there is enough space on it).  Not that this will always have enough
information but it is worth to try.

PWS500's seem to be particularly quirky.  Scanning through archives
of comp.os.linux.alpha you can find a lot of examples of weird
behaviour of these.

  Michal




