Dear Gary,

I thought I'd send this off-line.  I have been using a 500mhz Miata for
aboput a year now and have had Redhat 7.1, 7.2, Tru64 5.1 and 5.1a, NT
4.0, Windows 2000, and Free BSD running on mine at various times.
Overall i think it is a good machine and it seems as fast running Linux
as my XP2000 Athlon PC. In other words, not that fast.  It is a lot
quicker under Tru64 and NT.

The chips that run the IDE/Atapi drive and the floppy are probably the
Miata's weak point.  I've been told that the later models, the ones with
USB ports, are much improved.  My own approach is to move as much of
that stuff as possible onto SCSI, which feeds into the main bus and is
rock-solid. I had problems with the floppy that went away after i ran
the system 24 hours straight and then used the floppy drive.  Other than
that, my Miata has been a pleasure to use.

There are a few problems, not exactly related to hardware: first is the
different OSes have different relations to the system clock. If you
multiple-boot, your system clock is always getting whacked around and
Miata doesn't like that.

My own need was for a reasonably-priced workstation that would run GIS
application under Digital Unix.  I had hoped to be able to work on my
ArcInfo files under Tru64, then send them over to a FAT directory on
another hard drive and open then as coverages in ArcGIS 8.x running on
Windows NT or 2000.  Based on my experience, and primarily because of
the clock-whacking issue, i would say the best thing is to serve a
Windows2K box from the Miata, rather than try to do it all on one
machine.

I paid $400 for my pws500, but got a keyboard, mouse, a copy of NT with
certificate, Dragon Naturally Speaking, Corel Draw 8.0, and some
miscellaneous stuff.  I no longer run the MS product, but when i did, it
was easily the quickest NT box I have ever used. Quick as a rattlesnake
... way faster than any PC i have ever used, bar none.

For upgrades, I like the 3DLabs Oxygen VX1 video card,which you can get
for around $100 if you shop around, and a SCSI CDrom. My Miata had a
SCSI Zip drive which works great under Linux and NT, not so great under
Tru64.  I have never gotten a peep out of the on-board sound except
under NT/2K.  Someone else may have some suggestion as to what is a good
sound card for this machine.

I am not certain what DEC had in mind with this machine.  How it turned
out, it was the biggest,baddest PC of 1997-8. DEC was in the position of
selling lemonade in hell ... how they blew it is beyond me. Maybe if the
Miata had been backed up with about 100 really strong engineering apps?
Who knows?

On second thought I think I will post this.  There are a LOT of Miatas
out there and they seem to be selling on eBay in the $2-300 range now.
At that price I don't think you can go wrong. "Jay's computer parts"  is
a guy on eBay who seems to specialize in Miatas and always has a few.
My own thought is that if you want to get into an Alpha you can't do
much better at that price.

I like my Miata.  If you like yours, run it until it burns up. In a year
you'll be able to pick up another one "for parts" for about $100.

good luck

jn






