>> >I think Jay(?) did the Alpha support.  PCI support can be
>> >enabled on x86, but you have to change some bits in the
>> >driver and recompile.
>> 
>> You can do that, but it doesn't work.  I did so, and released it 
>> in RHL 7.3 to get back nothing but endless bug reports.  Ok, not 
>> endless, but you get the idea.  I have PCI Radeon hardware and 
>> plan on adding x86 support sometime in my spare time.
>> 
>
>I had not tried it myself.  I saw some recent discussion on DRI-devel      
>that seemed to indicate that it works for some.  In case it may     
>prove useful, these are the links to the discussion.
>
>http://sourceforge.net/mailarchive/forum.php?thread_id=1379924&forum_id=7177
>http://sourceforge.net/mailarchive/forum.php?thread_id=1380205&forum_id=7177
>http://sourceforge.net/mailarchive/forum.php?thread_id=1380575&forum_id=7177

Yep, I've seen that as well.  The problem with their idea of 
enabling this support broken or not until someone fixes it, is 
that all it really does is make distributions like Red Hat Linux 
get flooded with bug reports that are obscure.  By obscure I mean 
that I personally enabled it in Red Hat Linux 7.3, and got Radeon 
bug reports that I was unable to reproduce.  Unable to reproduce 
because Radeon PCI hardware is rather rare compared to AGP, and 
the PCI IDs of the cards are the same.  There was no easy way for 
me to tell that it was a PCI Radeon being used, and since I did 
not have such a card personally to test it with at the time, I 
disabled it again back to the stock XFree86 shipped code.

Re-enabling this by default in XFree86 is not going to *force* 
anyone to fix it.  It is going to instead just break a lot of 
currently working setups and cause distros to get flooded with 
bug reports.  If someone wants to fix it, they will.

I've now got 2 PCI Radeons graciously donated to me by ATI, and I 
plan on looking into this problem when I have some spare time, 
and hopefully fixing it, and doing so in a way that doesn't break 
something else.  I'm not going to do it right away though as I've 
got more important matters to attend to.  If someone enables this 
upstream to attempt to *force* the problem to be visible so that 
someone fixes it, I will just re-disable it via applying my patch 
in reverse.

Either people wait until I take a stab at fixing it, or someone
else jumps up to volunteer to do so IMHO.  Forcing known broken
code on everyone is just bad.  And this affects AGP boards too, 
because I got bug reports from people using AGP radeon that 
encountered the same problem.  What was happening was their 
kernel did not have AGP support, so it fell back to PCIGART, and 
died.

Forcing known broken code on people generates bug reports, 
because it is then an official "bug".  Right now, it is not a 
bug, but rather "PCI Radeon DRI is not supported feature yet".

At least in Red Hat Linux it will remain unsupported until myself 
or someone else has time to look into it and implement the proper 
support.  That is probably a minimal amount of work, since it 
obviously works on Alpha already.  It just isn't high priority 
currently.

Hope this helps understand the issue a bit better.


-- 
Mike A. Harris     ftp://people.redhat.com/mharris
OS Systems Engineer - XFree86 maintainer - Red Hat




