Hello,
On Thu, Dec 12, 2002 at 12:28:33PM -0500, terry.bowling@verizon.com wrote:
> One thing I forgot to ask.  I notice that when DiskDruid partitioned my
> system drive, it created partitions a, b, and c.  The c partition is just=
 a
> fragment of the disk just like parts a and b.  Under BSDlabel, shouldn't
> partition C point to the whole disk?  Will this cause a problem?

If you intend to share this disk with another Unix (actually, I only now it
for True 64) then C should indeed span the entire disk. Otherwise it
is completely irrelevant.

> I also notice that DiskDruid started partition a on cylinder 1.  Shouldn't
> it have started on cylinder 2 to leave that necessary gap for SRM?  It
> seems to be working fine, but I don't want to become dependent on this
> machine and find out later that I need to fix these items.

If you intend to boot of that disk, then you need space for aboot (SRM
has nothing to do with that) on that disk. Otherwise starting at
cylinder 1 is just fine. If you are currently booting then it works ?
(Sorry, I don't remember which drive you actually boot from).

