Ok, I'm back in business.  If it helps anyone, here's what I did:

I left my drive as a BSD label and ignored the c: partition issue, as well
as the blank space at the beginning of the drive as I'm not booting to it.
You don't actually have to specify the fd filesystem type.  Since my
/etc/raidtab file was already configured, all I had to do was type:
mkraid /dev/md0
mkfs.ext3 /dev/md0

This is a very handy way to make use of those multiple small disks you have
laying around, especially since these miatas have flaky ide controllers.
You can get 4gig scsi drives pretty cheap.

My setup is as follows:
/dev/sda (4GB)
   a:  /boot 128mb
   b:  /    *mb
   c:  swap 256mb

/dev/sdb (9GB)
   a:  swap 256mb
   b:  /data

/dev/sdc (18GB)
   a:  swap 256mb
   b:  /data

Should make a real nice samba server for our win2krap clients....

Thanks to everyone for your help.  If there are any other novices out there
that want to do something similar, it was surprisingly easy.  I'd be happy
to assist you.

----
Terry Bowling
