On Thu, Dec 12, 2002 at 12:18:39PM -0800, Eugene Chu wrote:
> I have a question regarding disklabels on Linux/Alpha.  When I use
> fdisk, I am given the ability to create either a DOS label or a DOS and
> BSD label.  Which one do I really need?

Any you want as long as it is supported by your kernel.  What
partitioning is used is only important for a boot disk and not because
of your Linux kernel but because a firmware expects some particular
disk layout.   You do not care if you are booting from a floppy
or CD (as then you do not have any boot hard drives :-).

> But doing
> a v in the DOS mode still causes fdisk to segfault.

Standard fdisk still has bugs for a situation when you have BSD
disklabel and you will try to switch with any operations to FAT one.
I reported that few years ago, and even provided a patch (true,
rather hacky one but a goal was to mess with fdisk internals as
little as possible) but no go so far.  The only sure way to switch
between partitioning types is to erase first sector or two using
'dd count=2 if=/dev/zero ... ' and write one on a clean slate.

  Michal




