On Mon, Dec 09, 2002 at 10:11:57PM -0600, G.L. Grobe wrote:
>
> RH 7.2 reported a successful install, so once the machine rebooted, I've
> tried several combinations of 'boot dkc0 -fl 0' and keep getting output
> similiar to the following ...
 
During the install, kernels and INITRD images should have been placed
in /boot. In addition, /etc/aboot.conf should have been created, with
appropriate options, one of which should be triggered by the "-fl 0"
of your first example.

Typing ">>> boot dkc0 -fl i" should give you an "aboot" message and
information aboiut how to see the options within /etc/aboot.conf.

> >>>boot dkc0 -fi /kernels/generic.gz initrd=/images/ramdisk.img -fl
> "root=/dev/hda expert text"
> device initrd=images/ramdisk.img is invalid

Again, the kernels and INITRD images are in boot; in addition, the
INITRD option MUST go within the -flags argument string. Also, I don't
understand why you're setting root to the CDROM? Is dkc0 == /dev/hda?
Or do you have an IDE disk in there?

Anyway, such a direct boot command should prolly look like:

 >>>boot dkc0 -fi 2/boot/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32.5 -fl "initrd=/boot/initrd-2.4.9-32.5.img root=/dev/sda2"

Now all that assumes that dkc0 is the first SCSI disk (hence the
"root=/dev/sda2"), and /root appears as partition 2 (hence the "-file
2/boot/vmlinuz-2.4.9-32.5").

You really have to describe your machine configuration a bit better to
give us more to go on.

Most MIATAs (PWSes) have IDE CDROM and SCSI harddisks.

 --Jay++

