My next personal hobby project is to write a script to install
RPMs from HP's updates area.  This message is a request for
comment on requirements and plans for the script.  (If anybody
else wants it, I'll be happy to post it when it's done, or at
least when it seems to be working well enough to be worth the
bandwidth.)

The basic precondition is there is a directory structure created
by something like "wget -rN" from HP's updates (or a mirror
thereof) containing a bunch of RPMs.  It appears the updates are
clumped in time so that interdependent RPMs have timestamps of
the same day.

The postcondition is that the appropriate RPMs will have been
installed.

In between is a little more complicated.  I don't plan to be too
fancy, in that I don't plan to compare the full 'capability'
lists provided and required by each package.  If there are
dependency issues not solved by the simple one-day-at-a-time
strategy, that will be left as an exercise for the reader.  :-)

Here are the plans so far:

  - I plan to code it in Perl.

  - It will be purely text-based and will query the user before
    removing or installing anything.  (That way, it's the user's
    fault if something goes wrong.  :-)

  - There will be three variables up front for customization:

    - the name of the base directory (either a path or symlink)

    - a list of subdirectory names under the base directory

    - a list of architecture types to install, in priority order;
      for example: 'alphaev67', 'alpha', 'noarch'

  - It appears the subdirectory name and the architecture type
    match, so I plan to issue a warning if that assumption is
    violated as a simple sanity check.

  - For a little bit of speed, I plan to make a hash of the
    architecture types.

  - Use "rpm -qa" to get the list of the packages already
    installed.

  - Scan the specified subdirectories to build a list of the
    packages available.  Do the suffix sanity check here.  Also,
    ignore any duplicates in different architecture types.  For
    example, if the list is ('alphaev67', 'alpha', 'noarch'), and
    if the same package base name is in both the 'alphaev67' and
    'alpha' subdirectories, ignore the latter one in favor of the
    former one.

  - Check the 'archives' directories to catch any that have been
    obsoleted since they were first downloaded.  With user
    permission, remove the .rpm files outside the 'archives'
    directories.

  - Subtract the already installed packages from the ones
    available to produce a list of candidates for installation.

  - Find the oldest file in the candidate list.

  - Starting at that date, for each date until all packages have
    been dealt with, do the following:

    - Find all files with timestamps in that day.

    - Run "rpm --checksig" on them.

    - Ask the user whether to proceed to install that packet.

    - Run "rpm -Uvh" on them.

What am I not understanding correctly about how HP's RPM updates
areas are structured, how RPMs should be installed and/or
anything else relevant?

Any other comments?

I guess to really test the script, I'll have to work up the
courage to install the RH7.2 CDs I bought back in July.  :-)

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net




