This is a follow-up to a request for comment on a script to
install Redhat 7.2 Alpha update RPMs from HP and mirror servers.

Many thanks to Bob Lindell <lindell@isi.edu> for suggesting
autorpm.  It looks like it has all the functions I need and then
some.  After a bit of fiddling, here's what I have come up with
for a first attempt at using version 3.1:

  - If you plan on only using local directories rather than FTP,
    and if you don't have the Net::FTP Perl module installed, you
    need to comment out autorpm.pl, line 25: 'use Net::FTP;' to
    make the Perl compiler happy.

  - To be able to play with it as a normal user without messing
    around in /etc/autorpm.d, change the setting in line 52 of
    $BaseDir to '.' or wherever you want to put your
    configuration files.

  - For convenience if using local directory storage for the
    RPMs, make short symlinks to the directories where
    the actual RPMs are stored.  In my case, I made links to
    alphaev67, alpha, and noarch.

  - Cobble together a configuration file, to be called
    autorpm.conf.  If using local directories, use the last
    example in docs/samples.conf (the last 16 lines of the file),
    with one repetition per architecture directory you want to
    use (alphaev67, alpha, noarch).

  - For convenience and to avoid future typos, make a short shell
    script to call autorpm.pl with --pidfile and --tempdir
    options.  Each one takes an argument, pretty intuitive.

  - Once in autorpm.pl, type an 'add' command for each
    architecture directory you are using, then type 'auto'.  It
    appears the 'auto' command attempts to do the installation.
    Actually, it appears the initial 'add' commands are not
    needed, but they are useful if you want to poke around the
    list to see which architecture flavor of things will be
    installed.

To know for sure how well it works, I guess I will have to break
down and install 7.2 on a test partition and give it a try.  Wow!
That was a whole lot easier than developing the script I was
preparing to write.

Thanks again to Kirk Bauer for creating autorpm, Bob Lindell for
recommending it, and others for other suggestions.

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net




