This is a second installment in observations about the use of
autorpm to install Redhat 7.2 Alpha update RPMs from HP and
mirror servers.

If you prefer (as I do, and as up2date does by default) to keep
old binary kernels around when installing newer ones, that will
require more coding in the autorpm.conf file.  So far, the only
thing I have found is

    Regex_Ignore("^kernel")

which will require kernels to be manually installed, during which
operation the kernel-* packages can be done with -U to remove the
old versions and the binary kernel package can be done with -I to
keep the old versions around.

When pulling RPMs by incremental 'wget -rN', packages that have
been moved to the 'archives' subdirectory won't be removed form
the local primary directory.  A quick script seems in order to
remove anything from the primary directory if it now appears in
the 'archives' subdirectory.

In a separate posting, I'm raising an issue about
gcc-java-2.96-112.7.2 not installing because it needs libgcj,
which appears to be missing from the updates area.  (It also
doesn't seem to be installed in the course of a normal
installation from CDs.)

Based on the almost complete success of using autorpm, I'm _not_
going to write that script I had mentioned.  Autorpm solves the
problem too well.

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net




