On Monday 23 December 2002 12:26 pm, Jean-Claude Magras wrote:
> Greetings,
>
> I have several alpha machines that I should probably bring
> back up but my real question is about Linux Raid which is
> probably seen more on Alpha's.
> A: How does the Emulex 7000 multimode sc fibre channel rate in
> support?

I wouldn't know.  I don't expect its Linux-Alpha support is all 
that great; that sort of support tends to only really shape up 
when it gets merged with the stock kernel.  Even 
enterprise-grade equipment is shifting to primarily support only 
x86 platforms. :(

> B: Any one ever used a 7 drive 2 power supply enclosure by
> Eurologic? The control panel and connections go between
> two fans and the direct opposite side the 9 drives and
> power supplies. No door. This fibre channel enclosure has
> only 1 DB-9 connection .

I've used a DPT Ultra2 RAIDStation (actually have it sitting idle 
at the moment), which is pretty much just a re-badged Eurologic.  
The old UltraWIDE model has the back arranged similar to what 
you describe, but the Ultra2/Ultra160 revisions are a bit 
different--three hot-swap fans lined up in a row, then a 
parallel row of two HD68 connections, with the environment 
monitor module between them. and the switches/power sockets 
clustered at the same end as the power supplies.

I don't think any of the SCSI cabinets have DB9 connectors though 
(sounds like a serial management port you would telnet into?)  
They tend to rely on the EuroLogic SAF-TE module built into the 
cabinet for reporting things like fan/PSU status in-band on the 
SCSI bus.

FWIW the cabinet is a very reliable, well-built piece of 
equipment and is capable of holding 1.6" drives.  It probably 
still works even today, and I've never had to swap any parts out 
of it.  It was a little expensive at the time tho ($2500 for the 
cabinet, then $110 for each of seven drive shuttles).  The fans 
can get about as loud as a vacuum cleaner if they decide to kick 
up to full speed.  It also has no filters on the air intake 
vents, so it sucks in a great deal of dust!

-- 
Kelledin
